%S A400401 1,8,6,9,2,8,5,5,8,6,8,3,8,8,4,4,0,6,1,4,9,4,3,8,7,2,9,2,1,1,0,2,1,5,
%T A400401 1,9,9,3,6,4,4,3,0,1,1,9,9,1,3,4,7,3,0,9,9,3,1,1,1,0,7,1,8,8,4,4,0,1,
%U A400401 5,6,2,5,4,6,6,2,1,2,9,7,1,7,6,3,1,3,2,2,1,3,8,8,9,1,1,3,4,2,9,2
%N A400401 Decimal expansion of e^(-3*T/2) * Sum_{k >= 1} I_k(T*sqrt(2))/2^(k/2), where T = 1.455378... is the constant given by A400400 and I_k is the order-k modified Bessel function of the first kind.
%C A400401 After an idea of Axel von Brömssen: Consider a football match where the two teams score according to independent Poisson processes with intensity 1 (expected number of goals per time unit) for the favorite team and 1/2 for the underdog team. Sequence gives the decimal expansion of the probability that the underdog wins when the length of the match maximizes this probability. A400400 gives the optimal match length.
%C A400401 In general, if the score intensity of the underdog is s < 1, the maximum probability equals e^(-(1+s)*T) * Sum_{k >= 1} s^(k/2)*I_k(T*2*sqrt(s)), where T is the optimal match length (see A400400).
%C A400401 Equals the maximum over t of P(S >= 1) where S has a Skellam(t/2,t)-distribution.
%H A400401 Pontus von Brömssen, <a href="/A400400/a400400.png">Plot of the outcome probabilities as functions of match length</a>.
%H A400401 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bessel_function">Bessel function</a>.
%H A400401 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skellam_distribution">Skellam distribution</a>.
%e A400401 0.18692855868388440614943872921102151993644301199134...
%o A400401 (Python)
%o A400401 from mpmath import mp,besseli,findroot,nsum
%o A400401 mp.dps = 110
%o A400401 t0 = findroot(lambda t:besseli(0,t*mp.sqrt(2)) - mp.sqrt(2)*besseli(1,t*mp.sqrt(2)),2) # A400400
%o A400401 p = mp.exp(-1.5*t0)*nsum(lambda k:besseli(k,t0*mp.sqrt(2))/(mp.sqrt(2)**k),[1, mp.inf])
%o A400401 print([int(d) for d in str(p)[2:102]])
%Y A400401 Cf. A400400.
%K A400401 nonn,cons,new
%O A400401 0,2
%A A400401 _Pontus von Brömssen_, Oct 07 2026
